docs/interface/tables-policy.md:60:The current game already separates outer page width from board overflow. Result data would have its own content and width behavior; it cannot simply inherit the card board's scroll logic by sharing a class name. A table's relationship between headers and values remains meaningful even when its viewport is narrow.
docs/interface/tables-policy.md:62:There is no table stylesheet in the source and no prescribed responsive table transformation here. A reviewer can still observe whether a proposed layout makes values readable and associated with their headers. Changing a collection into a stack of unrelated labels solely at a breakpoint can affect more than appearance, so both DOM and visual behavior are relevant evidence.
docs/interface/tables-policy.md:66:The existing accessibility journey covers game controls and completion, not a result table. Evidence for a future result view would identify its actual markup and readable rows; the current statistics tests cannot establish that structure. [Accessibility audit](../quality/accessibility-audit.md) provides the wider review context.
docs/interface/announcements-policy.md:13:The game has many visible updates, but only some represent a new result. A clock tick is a changing value; resolving a turn is an event. Treating those as the same kind of update would make a memory game noisy and would bury the information a player needs after selecting two cards.
docs/interface/announcements-policy.md:50:Two misses in a row produce the same sentence. Updating only a string state can leave the DOM text unchanged, which gives no new mutation for the second event. The keyed span avoids relying on a different visible sentence to distinguish them.
docs/interface/announcements-policy.md:98:A missing second identical result suggests checking event identity before changing the sentence. A repeated result on every clock tick suggests checking whether routine advancement touches the publication fields. A load notice visible near the textarea but absent from event output suggests checking whether the controller used `notice` or only changed local text.
docs/interface/form-layout-guide.md:82:This placement makes a paused-load outcome usable after the disclosure closes. It also means a selector that searches only inside details will not find Resume or the notice. Tests deliberately inspect the adjacent load-status location. [Error display guide](error-display-guide.md) follows its content and the load buttons' `aria-describedby` relationships.
docs/interface/status-bar.md:14:The status bar is a view of the current game and the current size's stored best move count. It does not run the clock, decide matches or write preferences. Keeping these jobs outside the component lets the same output describe ordinary play, a paused import and a completed import without separate counter logic.
docs/interface/status-bar.md:43:The function only formats a number. It does not advance the game or ask the browser what time it is. A clock render can therefore update the Time value without changing Moves, Pairs or the current turn output. The distinction is useful when debugging a report that the interface announces too often: the statistic and the turn feedback are different render paths.
docs/interface/name-length-policy.md:29:The policy chooses code points, not rendered glyph count or grapheme cluster count. A font can draw two code points as one ligature, and an input method can produce a composed-looking sequence. Neither observation changes which unit the check uses. Reviewing only a screenshot cannot establish that the correct unit was counted.
docs/interface/control-bar.md:20:The optional argument on `newGame` explains the two call sites. The select converts `event.currentTarget.value` with `Number(...)` and calls `newGame` with that value. New Game calls `newGame()` without an argument. The default in `use-game.ts` takes the current game's size. Restart calls its separate callback. There is no DOM inspection to decide which size to use when either button is pressed.
docs/interface/control-bar.md:42:The component itself receives `size: number`, but the session's `newGame` function checks `isValidSize(size)` before making a replacement. That guard is outside the control and also protects callers that do not originate in the native select. The option list is not the only validation boundary.
docs/interface/error-display-guide.md:11:This page explains how failed board-code and snapshot loads are reported without replacing the current game.
docs/interface/error-display-guide.md:71:Snapshot JSON has a 65536-character textarea limit and a UTF-8 byte bound in the decoder. Malformed JSON is only one failure kind. Unsupported versions, unknown properties, inconsistent matched positions, invalid selections and out-of-range counters can also produce the same UI error.
docs/interface/screen-reader-notes.md:14:The interface exposes native controls, card names, state changes and readable results. The repository README records accessibility-tree checks in Chromium 156.0.8078.4 with Playwright 1.64.1. It also explicitly records that no screen reader was run. The names and sentences below are source-backed content available to assistive technology, not a transcript of speech captured in a listening session.
docs/interface/screen-reader-notes.md:40:Board passes `face={null}` for a hidden card. Card then renders only the neutral back span with `aria-hidden="true"`, not the Face component. The hidden face is absent from the element's label, attributes and child artwork. A screen reader cannot legitimately read a face description from that hidden DOM because it is not there.
docs/interface/screen-reader-notes.md:42:This is stronger than hiding a face SVG with CSS while leaving a revealing title or data attribute. The browser input-contract test compares every hidden card's attributes, inner HTML and text against the expected neutral content. A visual-only inspection could miss such a leak.
docs/interface/screen-reader-notes.md:52:A first reveal starts elapsed time but leaves Moves at zero. The game has not yet completed a two-card attempt. It also does not publish a turn result merely for that first card. A user can inspect the newly focused control's name, move to another location and make the second choice.
docs/interface/screen-reader-notes.md:80:Invalid input keeps the current game and draft. The visible outcomes are `Invalid board code.` and `Invalid snapshot.`. The load buttons reference the nearby notice paragraph as a description, and the event feedback path publishes the outcome. Clicking a load button leaves focus there. An invalid Enter submission from Board code leaves focus in the input for correction.
docs/interface/reproduction-panel.md:35:Submitting prevents browser navigation. If the form value is a string, it passes it to `loadBoard`; only a true result triggers `loadButton.current?.focus()`. The ref belongs to the `Load board` submit button. This focus step matters when Enter submits from the input: a successful load replaces that input node, while a failed load leaves it inta…7781 tokens truncated…t, or publish invalid-snapshot notice |
docs/architecture/session-module.md:80:The exposed load methods return a boolean. `BoardCodeForm` uses that result to move focus only after successful loading. Snapshot loading is a button action on the current textarea string, so it does not replace the draft with a normalized serialization automatically.
docs/architecture/session-module.md:90:The hook applies an additional generation check before obtaining an observation. Its effect has an active flag and clears scheduled work at cleanup. Session rejection is the final data-level protection, not the only lifecycle defense. [Clock design](clock-design.md) details scheduling, visibility and fractional steps.
docs/architecture/session-module.md:100:The reader returns a complete fallback on invalid saved content, including an invalid entry within the best map. It does not salvage a subset of scores from a partly invalid preference object. The writer touches only its own key, and the store interface has no removal or enumeration method.
docs/architecture/clock-design.md:18:`browser-clock.ts` exports `CLOCK_INTERVAL_MS = 40`, `createClock` and `elapsedStep`. Its private `ActiveClock` interface contains only `observedAt: number`. It does not store accumulated active time, a mismatch deadline, a timer identifier or a pause flag.
docs/architecture/clock-design.md:20:`createClock(now)` returns `{ observedAt: now }`. `elapsedStep(clock, now, active)` subtracts the previous observation, accepts the delta only if active, finite and positive, and returns `{ clock: createClock(now), milliseconds }`. Accumulated elapsed time remains in `GameState`; the clock only supplies the next contribution.
docs/architecture/clock-design.md:103:An invalid load only changes notices. It neither replaces the game nor resets its baseline. An export during active play also does not pause. The controller's action semantics therefore determine which intervals count; the reproduction panel's visibility is not itself a clock state.
docs/architecture/typescript-settings.md:124:An application diagnostic commonly concerns component props, DOM events or game data consumed by the browser entry. A Node-project diagnostic can concern the CLI, source readers, build configs or unit tests. An end-to-end diagnostic can concern Playwright values, Node runner support or code executed in the page.
docs/competition/competition-rounds.md:54:A paper review can show several moments from an event instead of only its opening and final screens. One moment might show a board in progress, another an individual finish, and another a later match within the same round. The round names provide a stable point of reference across those images.
docs/competition/competition-boards.md:44:All face numbers used by the boards in this page are below 64, so they use the solid variant. The smallest board contains the eight shapes with the top marker. The middle board includes additional marker directions, and the largest reaches through four marker groups. This gives reviewers a more precise explanation of increasing visual complexity than saying only that there are more cards.
docs/competition/competition-ties.md:12:This page describes competition result comparison and explains the active-time information available from the current game.
docs/competition/competition-ties.md:42:## Active time in the current game
docs/competition/competition-ties.md:46:In the browser, `src/game/session/use-session-clock.ts` observes `performance.now()`. Its `running()` check combines visibility, pause state, the started flag and completion. `elapsedStep` in `browser-clock.ts` calculates the interval since the previous observation and contributes it only when active. This is elapsed active play, not a stopwatch started when the HTML file was opened.
docs/competition/competition-ties.md:68:These facts explain some apparent discrepancies in human timing. An observer measuring from the moment the device is handed over will include time before the first reveal. A wall stopwatch will include time while the page is hidden. A person looking only at clicks may overlook the visible thinking intervals between them. Those observations can all differ from `elapsedMs` without demonstrating a clock defect.
docs/competition/competition-ties.md:78:`src/game/move-cap.test.ts` covers completion at the move boundary. The browser and core clock tests cover elapsed behavior separately. Those tests are evidence about single-game values; the current source does not yet include a competition-specific result test.
docs/competition/competition-research.md:14:The sessions examined local play with the current game. The observations concern attention, learning and handoff.
docs/competition/competition-research.md:26:In the laptop session, Mara began with the pointer and described each revealed face aloud. Her first descriptions used only shape: circle, star, triangle. After encountering similar shapes with different markers in a larger practice board, she started adding direction. The vocabulary changed from “the star near the corner” to “the star with the marker on the right.”
docs/competition/competition-research.md:30:This observation agrees with the face model in `src/game/board/face-design.ts`: shape and marker direction both contribute to identity, and the game also includes a fill distinction. It also explains why the introduction in `Game.tsx` mentions shape and marker. A report that two faces “looked the same” can mean that a participant initially remembered only part of the available design.
docs/competition/competition-research.md:38:This was a useful correction to the observation method. Card navigation and memory retrieval are separate activities, even when they occur between the same two reveals. Counting only the delay between clicks would have concealed the difference entirely, since this participant was not primarily clicking.
docs/competition/competition-research.md:40:Leah also revisited an already matched card while moving across the board. She treated it as a landmark rather than as a card she intended to play. The current cards remain focusable in that state. That behavior made sense in the session, but an observer looking only at focus position might have misclassified it as a failed selection.
docs/competition/competition-research.md:74:A future research session could explore how different groups feel about spectating, but that is a research question rather than a committed feature. The existing observations support only the narrower conclusion that sharing a device also shares attention in ways that vary with posture, distance and familiarity.
docs/competition/competition-research.md:78:These sessions identified misunderstandings during local play. Shape-only memory, keyboard travel, device adjustment and the visible mismatch interval each explain a different kind of report. The observations are useful starting points for further sessions.
docs/competition/competition-deals.md:18:A match has a deal identity as well as the identities of its participants. Both players of a match play one board code, and every match in a tournament has a different board code. The identity in that sentence is the complete board code, rather than only the final numeric part or a screenshot of the visible board.
docs/competition/competition-deals.md:48:`newGame` passes the current game's seed to that function. It prevents the immediate replacement from reusing the previous seed, but it supplies no collection of earlier codes. A sequence can therefore revisit an older seed after an intervening solo game. Tournament creation in `src/game/tournament/fresh-deals.ts` tracks every full code for the event and chooses a distinct code for every match. Both players in a match receive the same code through the tournament controller.
docs/competition/competition-deals.md:50:That distinction matters because solo freshness checks only the immediately previous seed, while an event compares full board-code identity across every match. The tournament feature keeps that event-wide set in memory while it constructs the bracket.
docs/competition/competition-pairing.md:38:In this scope, the seed identifies a place derived from entry. It is not an estimate of memory skill. The current game has no account history, participant rating or service from which to obtain such an estimate. A developer looking for an existing ranking calculation in `src/game` will not find one.
docs/competition/competition-pairing.md:68:These boundaries are deliberate. The opening arrangement is small and explicit. General scheduling systems solve much larger problems, but importing their assumptions would add behavior beyond the current game and competition scope.
docs/competition/competition-scores.md:49:The first path is game replacement. `replaceGame` in `src/game/session/session.ts` copies preferences and assigns `size: game.size`. The ordinary browser actions for starting a new deal, restarting and successfully loading a board or snapshot use that replacement function. Its purpose is to keep solo play's chosen size in sync with the current game.
docs/competition/competition-scores.md:63:The distinction between values and writes is useful here. The competition sentence concerns the best-score and size values. It does not itself introduce a blanket prohibition on all storage API calls. A review that counts only writes can miss an in-memory change; a review that looks only at the current screen can miss a persisted change after reload.
docs/competition/competition-scores.md:81:A further comparison with ordinary solo play matters when reviewing an implementation. The existing solo behavior still provides the baseline: `tests/persistence-contract.e2e.test.ts` exercises better, equal and worse completed results across reloads, as well as successful and unsuccessful load effects on saved size. Those tests describe the current game; they do not exercise a competition screen.

